## CHAPTER TWO: LITERATURE REVIEW

### 2.1 Conceptual Review
Ride-hailing applications have transformed urban mobility. However, campus micro-transit systems require hyper-localized solutions. Existing apps like Uber or Bolt do not account for physical campus gates, student wallets, or virtual queues at designated faculties.

### 2.2 Empirical Review
Previous implementations of campus transport apps often suffer from poor adoption because they ignore driver resistance. This project pivots by making the app an administrative security requirement enforced by Student Affairs, rather than just a convenience tool.

